<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE bindings PUBLIC "-//NetBeans//DTD Editor KeyBindings settings 1.1//EN"
                          "http://www.netbeans.org/dtds/EditorKeyBindings-1_1.dtd">
<!--
    Toggle Word Wrap on VS Code's chord: Opt+Z on macOS, Alt+Z on Windows
    and Linux (View > Word Wrap is the same switch).

    Measured in the assembled cluster: Alt+Z is bound by nothing, editor
    or global, in any of the five profiles on any OS family, and no
    two-stroke binding starts with it. So this file rides all five.

    An EDITOR binding on purpose: on macOS Opt+Z also types a character,
    and only a press the editor's own keymap consumed can have that typed
    echo swallowed (editing/TypedEcho). A global shortcut would toggle the
    wrap and leave the character in the file.
-->
<!--
    The file's name ends in "-mac.xml" on purpose. The platform's keymap
    reader (KeyMapsStorage) logs a WARNING at every start for an Alt chord
    in the default profile ("may not work correctly on Mac") unless the
    file's name ends so: that suffix is how a file says its Alt was
    written with the Mac in mind. This one was: Opt+Z is the chord wanted
    there, and Alt+Z on Windows and Linux; the file applies on all three.
-->
<bindings>
    <bind actionName="nmox-toggle-word-wrap" key="A-Z"/>
</bindings>
